  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1138 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nUpper level high pressure is expected to stay in place over the \ncombined Panhandles through Monday, with temperatures remaining in \nthe upper 90s on the caprock to potentially 100-103 off the caprock. \nOnly a very slight chance for pops will be present through Monday. \nMonday night the upper level pattern is progged to shift with the \nupper level ridge moving east. This will lead to southwesterly flow \naloft and precip chances starting to pick up late Monday night. \nMonday night a 20 to 30 PoP is given for the northwestern combined \nPanhandles. However, Tuesday the high pushes even further east \nbringing a stream of Pacific and Gulf moisture into the area. With \nthis the NBM is giving a 60 to 80 PoP for the central to \nnorthwestern combined Panhandles. The far southeastern Panhandles \nmay be limited to around 30 PoPs at this time. \n\nEven Wed night the NBM is giving widespread 40 to 60 PoPs with the \nexception of the far southeastern Panhandles. PoPs remain in each \nsubsequent period through the rest of the extended period. With the \npotential rain, temperatures will be potentially lower in the low \n90s. Maybe even lower on some days. However, the NBM does not start \nintroducing the 80s for a high until Thursday, but in the upper 80s. \nIt is Friday when high temperatures in the lower to mid 80s start\ngetting introduced by the NBM.",
